Subject: QUANTUM: Suggestion

Dale Worley says:

> Conveniently, Bell's inequality is absolutely contradicted by the
> Copenhagen Interpretation, and it is (in theory) possible to construct
> an experiment to test it.  No experiment that fully satisfies the
> conditions of a showdown test has been done yet, but some fairly close
> approximations have been done, and the results have been in the
> Copenhagen direction.

This is misleading.  Aspect's experiments were the last serious physical
experiments to test the validity of quantum theory.  The results
validated quantum theory as a whole, not Copenhagen.  If Aspect had
obtained different results (and they had been repeated) the whole of
theoretical physics would have been thrown into a tizzy.

The validity of the various interpretations is being decided by gedanken
experiments.  As an example of gedanken experiments consider the quantum
computers of David Deutsch.  He shows that many-worlds is the only
possible interpretation, consistent with the maths, when you start
considering the existence of reversible, conscious quantum computers and
the interactions thereof.

> Personally, I would vote for the interpretation which expresses the
> apparent philosophical content of the mathematics over what seems
> "reasonable".

So would I - that's why my vote is for many-worlds.  Is that what you're
saying?
>                Over and over, the mathematics of physical theory has
> proved to be more correct than the intuition of the people who wrote
> the mathematics.

I agree - which is why I'm confused over your reporting of Aspect's
experiments.  Just to repeat: they don't support Copenhagen, but QM as
a whole.  Many-worlds is the only theory consistent with the maths and
it is a local, causal, relativistic theory with none of the
philosophical errors of the other interpretations.
> 
> Dale

