Subject: SELF: Anton's non-career

I said
> If I had been willing a decade ago . . . [to work] for Pentagon money, I
> might now be one of those high-paid coders.

I think I should expand on that.  Though I had five years' worth of credit
hours when I graduated, I changed majors about halfway and ended up with the
bare minimum of coursework in the field named on my diploma, Math & CS.  In
particular I had no courses in operating systems or compilers.  So my
opportunities were limited.  I spent four years on firmware for dedicated
8-bit micros, and couldn't convince anyone that I could do anything else.  I
figure that by "paying my dues" at Hughes for a few years, I might have
improved my resume enough to get the job of my dreams.

But that may be wishful thinking.  The holes in my education are not my only
defects as a software engineer wannabe -- I'm moody, feeble at interviews
(partly because my NT obsession with accuracy makes me come off as modest to
a fault), unconventional in appearance, socially clumsy, and lazy.

What would the Right Job be?  Not sure, but a couple of times in '85 I
almost got a job that should have been fun, at a startup.  Berkeley Systems
Works were doing something (I forget what) with voice synthesis, and
interested in my linguistics education; but they decided that they couldn't
afford the staff they already had, and I think were out of business in a
year or two.  (I loved the location: upstairs at Telegraph and Durant, a
block from Cody's.  I was living in Oakland.)  And I thought I hit it off
with Palantir (OCR), which promised some interesting math, but they couldn't
honestly fit me in.  (I remember the name of one of the people I talked to
there, by the way: Gayle Pergamit.)

Well, I spent *all* of 1985 job-hunting, and was laid off again in Feb 87; a
few months later I tossed my resume and gave away a bunch of computer books.

Jay asked:
> I too have friends who have turned down significant money to avoid working
> in the military. I respect them greatly for it. Do you now see it as a
> stupid, idealistic decision, (as I sometimes see my decision to go to
> *art* school)?

My snap answer was No, but it sorta depends on what you mean by stupid and
idealistic.  I spared myself some of the psychic strain of hypocrisy, but
only an idealist is bothered by hypocrisy and some might say it's stupid to
waste thought on it.

(I suffer enough pangs of hypocrisy from having been fed by a father who
never worked in the private sector, except for a summer at GE, and educated
in a public university.)

As DDFr said a few days ago, sometimes it helps to appear to be the sort who
does X, and one of the best ways to seem it is to be it.  And I don't
suppose you meet many anti-authoritarians working for Yoyodyne.

