Subject: D-FENS: Defending Ourselves

It seems to me that both Tim May and the Branch Davidians lack a plan
for "orderly retreat" as an essential component of defensive arrangements.
 
Ancient Castles often had a hidden tunnel leading to a nearby forest.
 
Pioneer cabins often had a basement "hidey-hole" accessed through a
hidden trap-door.
 
Both of these often saved lives and offered a path of escape when
other defensive measures finally failed.
 
In an urban setting, it might be feasible to dig a tunnel to the
nearest storm drain (weaken, but don't break the drainpipe until
actual need).
 
The Branch Davidians seem somewhat isolated. But a tunnel which came
up in a barn or near a ravine might offer a chance of escape under
cover of darkness.
 
Finally, to punish the enemy and confuse the trail, say 100 lbs. of
dynamite set to go off after the enemy has throughly occupied the
structure.
 
It seems to me that such arrangements should be made in structures
which are -likely- to be raided in force, such as crack houses.

