Subject: POLI: Operation Longarm

>Tom:
>> [child porn regulators'] argument: We are
>> attacking the demand side of the CP market because we want to affect
>> supply.  . . .

>How do you define a demand curve where the good demanded is not volume, but
>variety?  

>Anton Sherwood  

Hmmm . . .  I guess a good analogy would be with food.  One's demand for it
never ends, short of death.  CP consumers may have a similar demand for new
products.  The basic idea would be that the consumers of both food and CP
demand a constant supply.  Their demand curves are inelastic (not at any
moment, but across time).  

If CP demand is inelastic, then the economic approach to CP regulation will
have rather limited success.  Raising the costs of CP possession and
distribution will, as with the drug trade, merely give rise to a
high-profit, high-risk criminal industry.

