Date: Tue, 9 Mar 1993 09:40:09 -0500 (EST)
From: Edward J OConnell <ejo@world.std.com>
Subject: PHIL/ECON/NANO The End of Post-Scarcity

THE END OF POST-SCARCITY?

On Extropians from Jay at least! I've downloaded all the responses
so that I can dump them on paper and take a good a hard look at
them again. If anything comes to mind I think might be of
interest, I might post on this subject again; but I doubt it.
There's a lot of ego investment in scarcity here--it reminds me of
an anecdote of Kenneth Good's, from his book, Into the Heart, the
story of an American anthropologist's living with, and eventually
marriage to one of Yanomamo, the 'Fierce People.' (Good book! He
refutes a lot of Chagnon, by the way...)

Mr. Good brings the native woman who would become his wife to a
modern grocery store, and is amazed at the look of almost
existential horror on her face as she surveys the fantastic
profusion of foodstuffs. He then goes on to explain, that to the
Yanomamo, the question 'what would you like to eat?' is entirely
meaningless. The answer is always 'whatever you've got.' They
forage all day; pool the food, and eat it all, and are usually
still somewhat hungry. She is thrust into a situation, the
grocery, where her every assumption about the nature of the
universe is suddenly proven meaningless. Her life, and much of the
structure of its daily routine, is instantly obsolete.

She is terrified. And who wouldn't be?

These are all scary ideas.

I want to thank everyone for their time and effort in making
responses. I would also like to apologize for the slightly snotty
tone of the first essay; you should have seen version 1.0!

Tim May's assertion that there is nothing that can't be
unbreakably encrypted is a good one I think--except for the human
component of any such system. Recent E-mails from friends, and
friends of friends in the computer industry have been illuminating
in this regard. A financial programmer tells me of his Bosses
edict, banning all software piracy in no uncertain terms. The
Company, of course, bootlegs software for its own purposes, (as
will any entity, under sufficient market pressure, if the chances
of being caught are low and the rewards high) So my friend tells
the boss to go fuck himself, quietly, by continuing to steal, for
his own use, everything available on site, and making it available
to his friends; and to their friends, and to their friends... In a
world where software can build its own hardware a single
disgruntled employee may become a potent force for change. Again,
it doesn't matter if his disgruntlement is an honest, objective
response to his condition, or a shiftless, lazy, leftist whelp at
the Darwinian nature of the universe--the stuff is liberated.
Stolen.

Here, I must mention Perry's assertion that Bob Black's
motivation, and mine, in writing these things is simple
laziness--I've just cut out the flamebait and find I have nothing
non-sarcastic to say-- I guess I don't like being called lazy, for
some reason? Have I been corrupted by work ethic? I'll be
quivering in the grocery store of post scarcity with the rest of
you, I'm certain!

I agree with Nick Szabo's critique that Black speaks as if we're
post scarcity already, and we're not. Work a shift or three at
Burger King (I have) Scrub a few toilets. That's work, not play,
regardless of your attitude!

It has been my observation that Libertarianism; the FreeMarket,
Objectivism, miss the point on a lot human interaction--not that
they're invalid, but that they're not the all inclusive pictures
of human reality that they take themselves to be. I often wondered
why the pinnacle of selfishness, as described by Ayn Rand, wasn't
simple sociopathy--I'm reminded of the story in an abnormal psych
text, of a little boy smothering his infant sister because her
crying annoyed him. This, in my mind, is *true* selfishness!
Selfish people who design exquisite and utilitarian buildings as a
symptom of their selfishness I have little problem with...even
when they blow them up when ruined by committee...

As an artist and writer, so much of what I spend my time on has so
very little to do with money, per se. (Although you can be assured
it is about competition!) While I struggle like mad, to make my
pathetic 250$ for a single short story, (that money representing,
at this point, perhaps a thousand 'shiftless' hours of work) it
feels to me that the FreeMarket in some way misses the point, and
that vastly increased wealth could bring with it some fundamental
changes in the qualitative style of human interaction...at least
for people like me.

But heck, I'm just plain lazy, morally and intellectually, so what
the hell do I know? :)

It is my hope that one day, the majority of the human race will
find itself doing exactly what it wants to, as the elite of today
do; sharing the products of their 'labor' (Play?) in some sort of exchange.
That the treadmill changes in tone and flavor. That the net sum of
coercion--statist, marketbased, will decrease, over time. That
once we have assemblers and non-conscious AI to do the stuff
nobody likes doing, the whole game gets a lot more...

Polite? Fun? Better and More?

Jay

Signing Off on Post Scarcity

PS Ray Cromwell came up with a utilitarian reason for a post scarcity
marketplace I thought was interesting...that there will be things he
wanted to have done that would be dangerous, and that we'd have to pay
people to do them. (He gave as example, exploring a dangerous place)

He was tired when he posted, but even so, with consciousness back up,
I think people will be drawing straws to see who gets to do the most
dangerous 'work' in any society. Shit, I'd be a policeman, for a year or
two, if I could back myself up now and then, (and if I wasn't just
hounding pot smokers and prostitutes) just for the thrill of running
around with a gun in my hand! How many cop shows have we watched? Wouldnt'
you rather live one? (if we didn't just get one man, in the video game of
life...Sorry, even this liberal weenie see's very little evidence for
reincarnation..)




