Dale Worley responded:
>Depends on how exactly you want to do it.  There are a fairly large
>class of theories that have the properties that imply Bell's
>inequality: ...
>Conveniently, Bell's inequality is absolutely contradicted by the
>Copenhagen Interpretation, and it is (in theory) possible to construct
>an experiment to test it.  

In theory there exists this large class of theories.  No member of
this class has been picked out, made explicit, and widely proposed.
And none of these are interpretations of QM.  An interpretation of a
theory should be consistent with the predictions of that theory over
most of the know domain of application, though there is room for some
disagreement at the edges.  Any theory satisfying Bell's inequality
disagrees with QM predictions in a pretty substantial way, and cannot
reasonably be called an "interpretation" of QM.  More like a competitor.

Ray Cromwell writes:
>Interpretations are in the realm meta-physics. Just because the predictions
>of mathematics disagree with intuition does not mean philosophical
>interpretations based on mathematics are also correct.  Those
>interpretations are based on the same flawed intuition that the mathematics
