>I am not against _strict_ interpretations of mathematics that are testable
>or best (according to Occam), I am against creative interpretations (e.g.
>Von Neumann's "... and then a miracle happens."). However, I do feel that
>sometimes even strict interpretations get in the way of further scientific
>breakthoughs.

Huh?  Are you arguing against all use of intutition, or only against the use
of intuition in philosophy?  Or are you arguing against all inferences which
are not currently and stricly testable?  These all sound like pretty
indefensible positions to me.

