> > stupid, idealistic decision, (as I sometimes see my decision to go to
> > *art* school)?


Well, I suppose I should bare my soul and throw myself on the mercy of the
Extropian High Court.  I have worked for major defense contractors.  And
I have liked it.  In fact, I worked for several years at that most
heinous of defense contractor/think-tanks, the RAND Corporation (Hi, Dave K.).

What lured me there was the opportunity to receive generous pay for writing
huge military war games.  Being a game playing sort, this was like
being paid to be a cryonics patient, or to read the Extropian Reading List.
I must also say that I had at the time, and still have at weak moments, some
lack of confidence in the idea of a voluntary national defense.  I'll note
it my defense, however that even our esteemed DDFr has expressed some
slight misgivings in this area. Given that the Former Soviet Union was doing
some Nasty Things [tm] at the time, there seemed justification for ensuring
that our country, more specifically my house, was not placed under the 
jurisdiction of a Country Even Worse Than Our Own.

So before you paint everyone who has ever worked for the defense establishment
either a statist or a hypocrite, you may want to consider this anecdote
as evidence that there may be another position.

Currently, I work in the private sector.  I made a concious decision that
I would find a private sector job becuase: 1) with the cold war essentially
over, I do not believe that we are under the threat of attack and 2) I
like and believe in the profit motive as a mechanism for determining
how a company will operate, which was not really the case in the defense
sector, and I suppose 3) I don't think the defense sector is a secure
place to work long term, as is being borne out today.

I will agree that having 7 years of technical experience at TRW and RAND
made it a hell of a lot easier to land my current job, and that it made
it possible for me to command a significantly higher salary than I might
otherwise have gotten.  Does this keep me up at night, riddled with
libertarian angst?  No.  Do I consider myself a libertarian, even a voluntarist?
You damned betcha.

I stand ready to receive sentence.



